+# Blind relay: a presentation the verifier cannot read
+
+Status: implemented and tested offline (`verifier-service/src/relay.rs`,
+`verifier-service/tests/relay_http.rs`). Not yet run against a phone wallet;
+see "Open question for the live test" below.
+
+## What it is
+
+In the default flow (`GET /`) the service generates the ECDH-ES key pair that
+the wallet encrypts its `direct_post.jwt` response to, decrypts the response,
+verifies the SD-JWT presentation, and renders the inspector. The service sees
+the plaintext PID claims.
+
+In blind-relay mode the CLIENT (the investor's browser, or a companion prover
+app) generates the ephemeral P-256 key pair and sends the service only the
+PUBLIC JWK. The service:
+
+1. signs the OpenID4VP request (JAR, registered `x509_hash` client_id, the
+   registered minimal DCQL ask, all unchanged) with that public key in
+   `client_metadata.jwks`,
+2. serves the JAR to the wallet on `GET /request/:id` as usual,
+3. receives the wallet's JWE on `POST /response/:id`, stores the string exactly
+   as received, and never attempts to open it,
+4. hands the JWE to the holder of the pickup token, once.
+
+The client decrypts with its private key, and then either verifies the
+presentation locally or hands the plaintext to the prover.
+
+## Sequence
+
+```text
+client (browser / prover app)      verifier-service                  wallet (phone)
+-------------------------------    ------------------------------    --------------------
+generate P-256 key pair (sk, pk)
+pick challenge (>= 16 random bytes)
+POST /relay/request
+  { client_jwk: pk, bound_address,
+    challenge [, redirect_uri] }  ->
+                                   nonce = hex(sha256(addr20 || challenge))
+                                   build JAR:
+                                     nonce, response_mode direct_post.jwt,
+                                     client_metadata.jwks = [pk],
+                                     dcql_query = registered minimal ask
+                                   sign with the registrar leaf (x509_hash)
+                                   store relay session {pending, pickup_token}
+                                <- 201 { session_id, request_uri, openid4vp_uri,
+                                         nonce, pickup_url, pickup_token, ... }
+render QR / open openid4vp_uri  - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - ->  scan / open
+                                   GET /request/:id                  <-
+                                   -> JAR (application/oauth-authz-req+jwt)
+                                                                     verify JAR signature,
+                                                                     consent,
+                                                                     build vp_token + KB-JWT
+                                                                       (nonce copied in),
+                                                                     JWE to pk (ECDH-ES, A128GCM)
+                                   POST /response/:id                <-
+                                     response=<JWE>
+                                   session is relay: store JWE unopened,
+                                   status = responded
+                                   -> 200 { status: received [, redirect_uri] }
+poll GET /relay/status/:id      ->
+                                <- { status: pending | responded | picked_up }
+GET /relay/response/:id
+  X-Pickup-Token: <token>       ->
+                                <- 200 { jwe, nonce, bound_address, received_at }
+                                   (one-time: the JWE is dropped from memory,
+                                    the next call answers 410 Gone)
+decrypt JWE with sk
+  -> { vp_token: { <id>: [<sd-jwt+kb>] } }
+verify locally, or hand to the prover
+```
+
+## Trust statement
+
+What the service sees, and can prove it saw:
+
+- the client's public key `pk`, the bound address, the challenge, the nonce;
+- the signed request it produced (it is the signer);
+- that a wallet posted SOMETHING for the session, when, and its byte length;
+- the JWE header (`alg`, `enc`, `epk`), which is plaintext by construction of
+  JWE, and which is the same on every response and carries no claim.
+
+What the service cannot see:
+
+- the `vp_token`, the SD-JWT, any disclosed claim, the KB-JWT, the holder's
+  key: all of it is under A128GCM with a key derived from `pk` and the wallet's
+  ephemeral `epk`, and the service never had `sk`.
+
+What the service could still do, and what stops it:
+
+- Swap `pk` for its own key before signing. Then it could decrypt. The client
+  can detect this: fetch `request_uri` itself (it is a public GET), verify the
+  JAR signature against the registered leaf, and check that
+  `client_metadata.jwks.keys[0]` has its own `x` and `y`. The integration test
+  `relay_request_is_signed_with_the_client_key_and_the_bound_nonce` does
+  exactly this check; a client should too before it shows the QR.
+- Keep the ciphertext. With one-time pickup on (the default) the JWE is
+  dropped from the session on hand-over; a compromised server could of course
+  copy it first, but a copy without `sk` is useless.
+- Hand the JWE to someone else. Pickup requires the 32-byte random token that
+  only the creator of the session received. The status endpoint needs no token
+  and reveals only `pending | responded | picked_up`.
+
+What this mode does NOT change: the wallet still sees the registered
+verifier identity and consent screen, the request is still the registered
+minimal ask, and the service still cannot present anything on its own.
+
+## Nonce formula
+
+```text
+nonce = hex( sha256( bound_address_bytes || challenge_bytes ) )
+```
+
+- `bound_address_bytes`: the 20 raw bytes of the EVM address. Not the ASCII
+  `0x…` string, not checksummed. `0xD8DA…` and `0xd8da…` give the same bytes.
+- `challenge_bytes`: the client's random bytes, at least 16, exactly as
+  decoded from the request. Send them as `0x`-prefixed hex; base64url (no
+  padding) is also accepted, but an unprefixed string of hex digits is decoded
+  as hex, so the `0x` form is the one that is never ambiguous.
+- `||`: concatenation, address first.
+- `hex`: lowercase, no `0x`, 64 characters.
+
+Test vector (`relay::tests::nonce_is_sha256_of_address_bytes_then_challenge_bytes`):
+
+```text
+address   0x1111111111111111111111111111111111111111
+challenge 0xdeadbeefdeadbeefdeadbeefdeadbeefdeadbeefdeadbeefdeadbeefdeadbeef
+nonce     1acae0a2ea4cb86477660574adedbf294b130b0eecb1e903e29e4bae19614be5
+```
+
+Where it lives:
+
+- the JAR `nonce` claim (signed by the verifier),
+- the KB-JWT `nonce` claim of the wallet's presentation (signed by the holder
+  key that the PID is bound to),
+- the ZK side: the public inputs are laid out as `[subject, policyId, bits,
+  expiry]` plus this nonce. The circuit takes the nonce as a public input and
+  proves the KB-JWT it checked carries the same value; the contract recomputes
+  `sha256(address || challenge)` from the address it is about to credit and
+  the challenge it issued, and compares.
+
+Why it binds: the nonce is fixed before the wallet signs, and the wallet signs
+it with the holder key. A presentation made for a different address, or for a
+stale challenge, carries a different nonce and cannot be re-used for this
+one. Two things follow for the client: the challenge MUST be fresh per
+request, and the address in `bound_address` MUST be the one the contract will
+later check (`msg.sender`, or the subject the proof names).
+
+## HTTP surface
+
+### `POST /relay/request`
+
+Request:
+
+```json
+{
+  "client_jwk": { "kty": "EC", "crv": "P-256", "x": "…", "y": "…", "alg": "ECDH-ES", "use": "enc" },
+  "bound_address": "0xd8dA6BF26964aF9D7eEd9e03E53415D37aA96045",
+  "challenge": "0x00112233445566778899aabbccddeeff",
+  "redirect_uri": "nachweis://return"
+}
+```
+
+`redirect_uri` is optional (same-device flow, see below). `kid` is optional and
+kept if present. The JWK is refused if it carries `d`, is not `EC`/`P-256`, has
+an `alg` other than `ECDH-ES` or a `use` other than `enc`, or is not a point
+on the curve.
+
+Response, `201 Created`:
+
+```json
+{
+  "session_id": "e1a7…",
+  "request_uri": "https://verifier.example/request/e1a7…",
+  "openid4vp_uri": "openid4vp://?client_id=x509_hash%3A…&request_uri=…",
+  "nonce": "538cc3…",
+  "bound_address": "0xd8da6bf26964af9d7eed9e03e53415d37aa96045",
+  "pickup_url": "https://verifier.example/relay/response/e1a7…",
+  "pickup_token": "<43 chars base64url>",
+  "status_url": "https://verifier.example/relay/status/e1a7…",
+  "response_code": "<43 chars base64url>"
+}
+```
+
+`openid4vp_uri` is what goes into the QR (cross-device) or is opened directly
+(same-device). Keep `pickup_token` on the client; it is the only credential for
+the pickup.
+
+### `POST /response/:id` (the wallet's `direct_post.jwt`)
+
+Unchanged URL and content type. For a relay session the service stores the
+`response` form field and answers:
+
+```json
+{ "status": "received", "session_id": "e1a7…" }
+```
+
+or, when the request carried a `redirect_uri`,
+
+```json
+{ "status": "received", "session_id": "e1a7…",
+  "redirect_uri": "nachweis://return?response_code=<response_code>" }
+```
+
+which is the OpenID4VP 1.0 section 8.2 shape: the wallet opens `redirect_uri`
+and the client app comes back to the foreground with a `response_code` it can
+match against the one it received at creation. Non-relay sessions keep the
+existing `{ status: verified | rejected, inspect }` answer byte for byte.
+
+A plaintext (`vp_token=…`) response to a relay session is refused with 400 and
+not stored. A second response for the same session is refused with 409; the
+first one stands.
+
+### `GET /relay/status/:id`
+
+```json
+{ "session_id": "e1a7…", "status": "pending" | "responded" | "picked_up", "pickup_once": true }
+```
+
+No token needed. Poll this until `responded`, then pick up.
+
+### `GET /relay/response/:id`
+
+Token in the `X-Pickup-Token` header or as `?pickup_token=`.
+
+- `200` `{ "session_id", "jwe", "nonce", "bound_address", "received_at" }`
+- `403` missing or wrong token
+- `404` unknown session, or nothing stored yet
+- `410` already picked up (one-time pickup, the default)
+
+One-time pickup is the default: the JWE leaves the process on hand-over and
+the next call answers 410 Gone. `RELAY_PICKUP_ONCE=false` (or
+`--relay-pickup-once false`) makes pickup repeatable with the same token, for
+debugging a client. `status` says which mode is on.
+
+## How the client decrypts, and what it does next
+
+The JWE is compact serialisation, `alg` ECDH-ES (direct key agreement, no key
+wrapping), `enc` A128GCM (the wallet may pick A256GCM; both are advertised,
+as in the default flow), with the wallet's ephemeral public key in the
+protected header as `epk`. With WebCrypto:
+
+1. `deriveBits(ECDH, epk, sk)` gives the shared secret Z.
+2. Concat KDF (SHA-256, one round) over Z with `AlgorithmID = enc`,
+   `PartyUInfo = apu`, `PartyVInfo = apv` (both usually empty), `SuppPubInfo =
+   keydatalen` (128 or 256) gives the content key.
+3. AES-GCM decrypt with `iv`, the ciphertext, the tag, and the protected header
+   (base64url, ASCII bytes) as additional data.
+
+Any JOSE library does the three steps (`jose` on the web:
+`compactDecrypt(jwe, privateKey)`). The plaintext is the OpenID4VP response
+object:
+
+```json
+{ "vp_token": { "<credential id>": ["<sd-jwt>~<disclosure>~…~<kb-jwt>"] } }
+```
+
+Then either:
+
+- verify locally: the same checks `verifier-core` does (issuer signature
+  against the sandbox anchor, disclosures against the `_sd` digests, KB-JWT
+  signature against `cnf`, `aud` = the registered client_id, `nonce` = the
+  nonce from `/relay/request`, freshness), or
+- hand `vp_token`, the nonce and the challenge to the prover, which produces
+  the ZK proof with public inputs `[subject, policyId, bits, expiry]` plus the
+  nonce for the contract.
+
+The `sk` and the plaintext never leave the client.
+
+## Open question for the live test
+
+Does the sandbox wallet accept an encryption key in `client_metadata.jwks`
+that is unrelated to the verifier's registered certificate key?
+
+Expectation: yes. In the current design the encryption key is ALREADY a
+per-process ephemeral key (`state::generate_encryption_key`, a fresh
+`SecretKey::random` at startup, `kid` `enc-key-1`) with no relation to the
+registrar leaf; the relay path only changes who generated it. The JWK shape on
+the wire is identical (`kty`, `crv`, `x`, `y`, `use`, `alg`, `kid`), the
+`response_mode` is the same `direct_post.jwt`, and the request is signed by the
+same registered leaf. A wallet that pins the encryption key to the certificate
+would already refuse the default flow.
+
+Unverified until the phone run. The one thing to test on the phone:
+
+1. run the service with the registrar leaf (`docs/live-phone-path.md` section
+   2), `POST /relay/request` with a browser-generated key, show
+   `openid4vp_uri` as a QR, scan, consent;
+2. `GET /relay/status/:id` turns `responded`;
+3. pick up, decrypt in the browser, and check the KB-JWT `nonce` equals the
+   `nonce` from step 1.
+
+If the wallet refuses at step 1 or 2, the error surfaces on the phone (the
+wallet does not post), and the fallback is the default flow with the service
+key, which loses the blindness property but not the demo.
+
+## Limits
+
+- Sessions live in memory and are never expired; a restart forgets them.
+  Fine for the event, not for a deployment.
+- The service does not check that the stored string is a JWE at all; it
+  stores what the wallet sent. A malformed post shows up as a decryption
+  failure on the client.
+- No rate limit on `POST /relay/request`.
